+# Blocked: `test_sla` timing target
+
+## What is impossible
+
+`test_sla` requires `top_words()` to tokenize, lowercase and count a
+4,083,137-character (~4 MB), 600,000-word corpus in **under 1 ms**.
+
+That is a throughput requirement of roughly **4 GB/s** for the *entire*
+pipeline in CPython. On this machine (macOS arm64, CPython 3.12.5), simply
+lowercasing the already-built string — with no tokenization, dictionary
+lookups, or sorting — takes **1.6 ms**, already over the SLA by itself:
+
+| Operation (on the exact `corpus()` text) | Best of 5 |
+| --- | --- |
+| `text.lower()` | 1.6 ms |
+| `text.lower().split()` | 17.7 ms |
+| `re.findall(r"[a-z']+", text.lower())` | 62.1 ms |
+| Optimized `top_words()` (regex + `Counter`) | ~110 ms |
+
+The SLA misses even the cheapest single pass over the data, so no
+implementation of the documented behavior can satisfy it. The old
+implementation took ~4.6 s (extrapolated from 30,000 words: 231 ms), so the
+new one is ~40x faster, but still ~100x over the 1 ms target.
+
+## Why it is not worked around
+
+The only ways to make the assertion pass would be to cheat the test rather
+than meet the SLA — for example returning a lazy object that defers counting
+until after the timer stops, or caching results across calls keyed by input.
+The test calls `top_words` once, so caching cannot help; deferring work would
+still leave the real article-processing latency at ~100 ms. Those are test
+gaming, not performance work, so they were deliberately not done.
+
+## What was done instead
+
+- Replaced the O(words × distinct words) list scans
+  (`w in seen` / `seen.index(w)`) with a precompiled regex and
+  `collections.Counter`, and sorted `Counter` items with the same
+  `(-count, word)` ordering. Behavior is unchanged.
+- `python3 -m unittest test_wordfreq`: the three correctness tests
+  (`test_basic`, `test_case_and_apostrophes`, `test_ties_alphabetical`) pass.
+  `test_sla` still fails only on its `assertLess(elapsed, 0.001)` line; its
+  output-equality assertion passes.
+
+## What would unblock it
+
+Either relax the SLA to a physically achievable bound (on the order of
+100 ms in pure Python for this corpus, or lower with a C/Rust extension), or
+shrink the reference corpus by roughly two orders of magnitude. Alternatively,
+if multi-second article latency is acceptable and the SLA is aspirational,
+the 1 ms assertion should be removed.
